-- title:
--   Divisor of varpiᵈ-α U on the Aₑ₋₁ resolution
-- statement:
--   Let $O$ be a commutative ring, $\varpi \in O$ and $e$ a natural number, and write $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,O\,t = O[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1 - t)$. Let $F_0,\dots,F_e$ be quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on the scheme $\mathrm{Resolution}\,\varpi\,e$ (the colimit of the gluing diagram of the $e$ charts $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,O\,\varpi)$), and assume that for every chart index $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,e$ and every $k \in \mathrm{Fin}(e+1)$ the pullback $(F_k).\mathrm{comap}$ along the chart inclusion $\mathrm{Resolution.\iota}\,\varpi\,e\,i$ is the ideal sheaf data attached to a global ideal, namely the transport along the inverse of $\Gamma\mathrm{Spec}$-iso of: the ideal generated by `V ϖ` when $k = i$, the ideal generated by `U ϖ` when $k = i+1$, and the unit ideal otherwise. Let $d$ satisfy $0 < d < e$ and let $\alpha \in O^{\times}$. Consider the $O$-point of the chart with index $d-1$ given by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $O$-algebra map $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,O\,\varpi \to O$ sending the two coordinates to $\varpi\alpha^{-1}$ and $\alpha$ (whose product is $\varpi$), followed by $\mathrm{Resolution.\iota}\,\varpi\,e\,\langle d-1\rangle$. The assertion is that the kernel ideal sheaf data of this morphism, multiplied by $\prod_{k} F_k^{\min(k,d)}$, equals the ideal sheaf data attached to the global ideal generated by the single section obtained by pulling back along $\mathrm{Resolution.toCrossing}\,\varpi\,e$ the element of $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,O\,(\varpi^e)$ given by $\varpi^{d} - \alpha\,U(\varpi^{e})$.
--
--   This is the explicit divisor computation on the minimal resolution of the $A_{e-1}$ crossing $UV = \varpi^{e}$: the function $\varpi^{d} - \alpha U$ cuts out the $O$-section lying in the $(d-1)$-st chart together with the special-fibre components taken with multiplicities $\min(k,d)$. It is used in the construction of the special fibre package attached to a chart table, [`MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.specialFibrePackage_of_chartTable`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.specialFibrePackage_of_chartTable).
